WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.580
As with most things in machine knitting

2
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:07.490
there are many ways to add
a folded band to a sweater.

3
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.100
Let's take a look at one method
of adding a plain stockinette

4
00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.580
band to some fairisle knitting.

5
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.660
This sample represents the front of

6
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.260
a cardigan. In a real project.

7
00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:20.420
For accuracy, I would measure the front

8
00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:25.780
edge and then I'd establish
my stitch gauge in stockinette.

9
00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.940
Multiply the length of the front
by my stitch stitch gauge.

10
00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:33.220
And I'll know exactly how
many stitches to pick up.

11
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.280
For this sample, I'm simply holding
the knitting up to the needle bed, and.

12
00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:41.860
I'll pick up approximately three
stitches for every four rows.

13
00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.060
With practice, you'll be able to eyeball.

14
00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.740
This. With the WRONG SIDE facing.

15
00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.740
I'll hang both edges and then the center.

16
00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:56.780
I'll continue to divide the sections
in half, hanging the stitches neatly.

17
00:00:56.810 --> 00:01:00.460
Be careful when picking up.

18
00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:04.930
I didn't look closely before I started
knitting, and you can see the hole.

19
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:07.210
This was caused by hanging the same.

20
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.770
Space in the knitting on two
needles side by side.

21
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.620
So don't do as I did.

22
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:18.160
Double check the stitches
before you start knitting.

23
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:23.820
Grading the band is helpful
in keeping it from flipping out.

24
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.140
Knit the inside or the private side
of the band at a slightly tighter.

25
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.850
Tension than the outside
or the public side.

26
00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:37.800
This will force the band to curl
towards the inside slightly.

27
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.180
Knit the first

28
00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:46.780
section of the band,
and then knit a turning row.

29
00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:48.680
I just knit one row at a

30
00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:52.880
very loose tension to create a crease.

31
00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.880
Then knit the second side of the band.

32
00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.920
Let's look at two ways to secure the band.

33
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:15.920
First, pick up the edge stitches
and hang them on the needles.

34
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.860
And loosely bind off these stitches.

35
00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.100
Now, I don't prefer this method.

36
00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:40.660
It adds bulk and it's difficult
to get the bind off just right.

37
00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.140
Not too loose and not too tight.

38
00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:48.740
The second method is to scrap
off the band stitches.

39
00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:50.720
Since I'm going to be hand stitching.

40
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.900
Those live stitches, I knit a row

41
00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.180
Of ravel cord and then
scrapped off in yarn.

42
00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.300
I find it easier to stitch from.

43
00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.580
The cord instead of from the yarn.

44
00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:06.660
With the live stitches secure, it's time
to close up this section of the band.

45
00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:11.720
I'll just whip stitch those
live stitches in place.

46
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:22.480
I suggest you practice both
methods and see what you prefer.

